#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""lane-lock — mechanical per-repo/lane ownership claim + gate-fail circuit
breaker (L-0310, "Build lane-lock + gate-fail circuit breaker", the operator
approved 2026-09-15).

WHY THIS EXISTS (gap this ticket asked for, confirmed missing on 2026-09-27
before this was written — see hub/reports/hub/2026-09-27-l0310.md):

  - LANES.md's "one owner per repo at a time" was enforced only by a human/
    agent reading free text, never mechanically. The 2026-09-21 21:19
    collision (two `npm run build` in one <product-a> worktree, root cause
    logged in LANES.md as "the ledger said owner: unowned, so nothing on
    disk said the lane was taken") is the exact failure mode a real lock
    would have caught before the second build ever started.
  - Nothing tracked a FAIL streak on bug-gate/playtester results per lane,
    so nothing could mechanically stop a worker from retrying a deploy into
    a lane whose last two gate results were both FAIL. the operator's own rule
    ("A failed bug check or UI check blocks the ship, even for something
    the operator asked for" — feedback_three_gate_agents.md) was honored only by
    each gate agent's own report, never enforced by a shared switch that
    later claims must check.

This tool is deliberately narrow and reuses existing conventions rather than
inventing new ones:
  - Dead-holder self-heal (a lock whose owner pid no longer resolves is
    cleared automatically) — same pattern as hub/bin/ws-build-slot and
    hub_lease_lib.py's pid/heartbeat dead-check, just pid-liveness only
    (no heartbeat staleness window) since a lane claim has no expected
    heartbeat cadence the way the single-hub lease does.
  - Alarm line on trip goes to delegation-alarms.log, the SAME file
    hub_lease_lib.append_alarm() already writes to for lease anomalies —
    one alarm surface, not a second one.
  - Every path is env-overridable (LANE_LOCK_ROOT, LANE_LOCK_ALARMS_LOG,
    LANE_BREAKER_THRESHOLD) so tests never touch production state — same
    convention as disk-guard's DISK_GUARD_VOLUME / hub_lease_lib's
    HUB_LEASE_PATH.

WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: it does not replace ws-build-slot (that's a machine-
wide BUILD CONCURRENCY semaphore, one slot, any lane) or hub-claim (that's
the single-hub SESSION lease, exactly one global holder). This is a named,
per-LANE (repo/ticket-scope) ownership + gate-health switch. A lane name is
just a string the caller picks (e.g. "app-a", "app-b",
"app-c") — this tool has no opinion on what lanes exist.

BREAKER SEMANTICS (deliberately manual-reset, not self-healing on one PASS):
  - record-fail increments a per-lane fail_streak. At >= threshold
    (default 2, LANE_BREAKER_THRESHOLD) the breaker TRIPS: tripped=true,
    logged to delegation-alarms.log, and every subsequent `claim` on that
    lane is REFUSED (exit 3) until an explicit `reset`.
  - record-pass resets fail_streak to 0 but does NOT clear an existing trip
    by itself — matching "a failed gate blocks ship even after the operator asked
    for it" (feedback_three_gate_agents.md): one later pass is not itself
    the hub reading the code, so it cannot be what silently reopens the
    lane. Only `reset` (naming who and why) clears a trip.

DESIGN DECISIONS from the 2026-09-27 bug-gate FAIL (report:
hub/reports/2026-09-27-buggate-l0310.md) — fixed here, each with a new
regression assertion in test-lane-lock.sh:

  1. `--pid` is now REQUIRED on `claim` (chosen over "default to the PPID
     chain's claude/node pid"). Reasoning: the bug was that an omitted
     --pid silently recorded lane-lock's own transient CLI pid (already
     dead the instant the command returns), so a second claim self-healed
     a second later with zero refusal. Walking the parent-process chain to
     find a claude/node ancestor is strictly MORE fragile, not less: it
     needs a `ps`/proc-walk that can itself fail or find the wrong
     ancestor (e.g. a caller invoked through a wrapper shell script whose
     own parent is bash, not claude), and a wrong guess would be silently
     wrong in the same way the bug already was. Required + a clear error
     is mechanically simple, fails loud instead of guessing, and every
     real caller (a build job, a hub worker) already knows its own pid.
  2. A corrupt breaker.json now fails CLOSED: `read_breaker_failing_closed()`
     treats any unparseable breaker.json as an already-TRIPPED breaker
     (blocking claims) rather than the previous silent default of
     tripped=False, and prints a WARNING plus appends one line to
     delegation-alarms.log every time it happens. Corruption should never
     be able to look identical to "healthy and untripped."
  3. `_CritSection` now (a) detects a wrong-type path at the critsec lock
     location (e.g. a plain file where a directory is expected) and fails
     immediately with a clear `CritSectionError` instead of looping
     forever trying `rmdir()` on a file, which always raises
     `NotADirectoryError` — caught by the old bare `except OSError: pass`
     — forever; and (b) after one steal attempt on a genuinely stale
     directory lock, if the lock STILL cannot be acquired, raises instead
     of retrying without bound. "Bounded wait" now means the call always
     returns or raises within a bounded time, never hangs.
  4. Pid liveness alone can't tell a live claim-holder from a different
     process that later reused the same pid. `claim` now also records a
     `start_key` (the holder's process start time, from `ps -o lstart=`)
     at claim time; `pid_is_same_process()` compares BOTH liveness and
     that start_key before treating a recorded holder as still the real
     holder. A pid that's alive but whose start time no longer matches is
     treated the same as a dead pid (self-heals). When no start_key was
     ever captured (e.g. `ps` unavailable), this falls back to the old
     liveness-only check rather than manufacturing a false refusal.
     `force-release <lane> --by <name> --reason <text>` is the new,
     explicit, always-available mechanical override for a lane stuck by
     PID reuse (or anything else) — it logs who released it and why to
     delegation-alarms.log, the same way `reset` logs breaker overrides.

FURTHER FIXES from the 2026-09-27 RE-GATE r2 FAIL (report:
hub/reports/2026-09-27-buggate-l0310-r2.md) — fix #4 above had two real
gaps, closed here, each with a new regression assertion:

  1. (blocker) `ps -o lstart=` is TZ- and locale-dependent: the SAME live,
     unchanged process produces a different string under TZ=UTC than
     under TZ=America/Chicago, so a claim and a later check running under
     different env silently declared a genuinely alive holder dead and
     stole its lane — reopening the exact 2026-09-21 double-build
     collision this ticket exists to prevent, via a new vector. Fixed by
     pinning TZ=UTC/LC_ALL=C/LANG=C via subprocess's own `env=` (never by
     mutating this process's os.environ) on every `ps` call — both the
     write path (claim, recording start_key) and the compare path
     (pid_is_same_process recomputing it fresh) go through the one
     proc_start_key() function, so there is only one place this could
     drift. A fingerprint is now a tagged {"fmt": START_KEY_FMT, "value":
     ...} dict, never a bare string, so an OLDER fingerprint (written
     before this fix, under whatever TZ/LANG the caller happened to have)
     is always distinguishable from a current one. A format mismatch is
     NEVER treated as proof of death — pid_is_same_process() treats it as
     UNKNOWN identity, assumes the holder is still alive, and logs an
     alarm so the legacy fingerprint is visible and can be refreshed by a
     re-claim, rather than risking either a false steal or silently
     trusting a truly different process.
  2. (blocker) A corrupt owner.json was NOT fail-closed — the exact same
     defect class fixed for breaker.json (fix #2 above) but never
     extended to the sibling file, and the more important of the two for
     this tool's actual purpose (mutual exclusion). Fixed the same way:
     `read_owner_failing_closed()` treats an unparseable owner.json as
     held by an UNKNOWN owner (refusing claim AND release, pointing at
     `force-release` as the recovery path) instead of the previous silent
     default of "unheld." `force-release` deliberately does NOT go
     through this fail-closed reader — it is the one command required to
     work even when the file can't be parsed at all.
  3. (nit) `--pid` must now be a positive integer; `--pid 0` or a
     negative value is rejected with a clear error before any write,
     instead of quietly claiming with zero real liveness protection.

Usage:
  lane-lock claim <lane> --owner <name> --pid <pid> [--reason <text>]
  lane-lock release <lane> --owner <name>
  lane-lock force-release <lane> --by <name> --reason <text>
  lane-lock status <lane>
  lane-lock list
  lane-lock record-fail <lane> --ticket <id> --gate <bug-gate|playtest> [--detail <text>]
  lane-lock record-pass <lane> --ticket <id> --gate <bug-gate|playtest>
  lane-lock reset <lane> --by <name> --reason <text>

Exit codes: 0 ok, 1 usage/internal error (including a missing/non-positive
required --pid, or a critsec-lock timeout/wrong-type-path), 2 lane held by
another live owner (including a corrupt-owner.json fail-closed, which
looks the same to a caller as "held by someone" — see `force-release`), 3
breaker tripped (claim refused, including a corrupt-breaker fail-closed).
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import os
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from datetime import datetime, timezone
from pathlib import Path

HOME = Path.home()
ROOT = Path(os.environ.get("LANE_LOCK_ROOT") or (HOME / ".claude/hub/locks/lanes"))
ALARMS_PATH = Path(os.environ.get("LANE_LOCK_ALARMS_LOG") or (HOME / ".claude/hub/delegation-alarms.log"))
THRESHOLD = int(os.environ.get("LANE_BREAKER_THRESHOLD") or 2)
LOCK_WAIT_S = float(os.environ.get("LANE_LOCK_WAIT_S") or 5.0)


def now_iso() -> str:
    return datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat(timespec="seconds")


def pid_alive(pid) -> bool:
    """Liveness only: 'does *some* process currently have this pid.' Does
    NOT check identity — a crashed holder's pid can be reused by an
    unrelated process, which this function alone cannot detect. Callers
    that need to know whether the ORIGINAL claim-holder is still alive
    must use pid_is_same_process() instead (finding #4, 2026-09-27
    bug-gate)."""
    try:
        pid = int(pid)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return False
    if pid <= 0:
        return False
    try:
        os.kill(pid, 0)
        return True
    except ProcessLookupError:
        return False
    except PermissionError:
        return True  # exists, just not ours — still alive
    except OSError:
        return False


# Format tag for the identity fingerprint written by proc_start_key(). Bump
# this if the fingerprint's shape or the `ps` invocation ever changes, so an
# old fingerprint written under a different scheme is never compared
# byte-for-byte against a new one (2026-09-27 re-gate r2, blocker #1: a
# bare, unpinned `ps -o lstart=` string is TZ/LANG-dependent — a claim under
# TZ=America/Chicago and a check under TZ=UTC produced two different
# strings for the SAME live, unchanged process, which silently self-healed
# a lane out from under its real holder).
START_KEY_FMT = "lstart-C-UTC-v1"

# Pin TZ/LC_ALL/LANG for every `ps` call this tool makes, on BOTH the write
# path (claim, recording start_key) and the compare path (pid_is_same_process
# recomputing it fresh) — both funnel through this one function, so there is
# only one place this can drift. Passed via subprocess's own `env=`, scoped
# to this one call, rather than mutating this process's os.environ (which
# would leak into anything else this process later shells out to).
_PINNED_PS_ENV_OVERRIDES = {"TZ": "UTC", "LC_ALL": "C", "LANG": "C"}


def proc_start_key(pid) -> dict | None:
    """Best-effort identity fingerprint for `pid`: its process start time
    from `ps -o lstart=`, run under a pinned TZ/LC_ALL/LANG so the exact
    same live process always yields the exact same string regardless of
    the CALLING process's own environment. Two unrelated processes that
    happen to reuse the same pid essentially never share the same start
    time to the second, so (pid, start_key) is a much stronger identity
    check than pid liveness alone. Returns a small tagged dict
    {"fmt": START_KEY_FMT, "value": <ps output>} — never a bare string —
    so a fingerprint written by this exact scheme is always
    distinguishable from one written by any other (older or future)
    scheme. Returns None when the pid can't be found or `ps` can't be
    run — callers must treat None as 'cannot verify', never as a match or
    a mismatch."""
    try:
        pid = int(pid)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return None
    if pid <= 0:
        return None
    env = {**os.environ, **_PINNED_PS_ENV_OVERRIDES}
    try:
        out = subprocess.run(
            ["ps", "-o", "lstart=", "-p", str(pid)],
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
            timeout=2,
            env=env,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    if out.returncode != 0:
        return None
    value = out.stdout.strip()
    if not value:
        return None
    return {"fmt": START_KEY_FMT, "value": value}


def _is_recognized_start_key(x) -> bool:
    """True only for a fingerprint in the CURRENT tagged format. A bare
    string (the pre-fix format, TZ/LANG-dependent) or any dict tagged with
    a different/unknown fmt both return False — callers must treat that
    as 'cannot verify', never as a mismatch, so a legacy or foreign-format
    record is never mistaken for proof that the holder died."""
    return (
        isinstance(x, dict)
        and x.get("fmt") == START_KEY_FMT
        and isinstance(x.get("value"), str)
        and bool(x.get("value"))
    )


def pid_is_same_process(pid, expected_start_key) -> bool:
    """True only when `pid` is alive AND (when we have a start_key to
    check against, in the current recognized format) its current start
    time still matches the one recorded at claim time. A pid that is
    alive but whose start time has changed is a different process wearing
    the old holder's pid — treated the same as dead (self-heals).

    With NO expected_start_key on record (an older claim from before this
    field existed, or `ps` was unavailable at claim time), falls back to
    liveness-only, same as always.

    With an expected_start_key that exists but is NOT in the current
    recognized format (2026-09-27 re-gate r2, blocker #1: e.g. a bare
    string written by the pre-TZ-pinning code) — this must NEVER be
    treated as a mismatch (that would silently declare a genuinely alive,
    unchanged holder dead purely because of a format/env difference, the
    exact defect just fixed). It is treated as UNKNOWN identity: assume
    still alive, and log an alarm so the legacy fingerprint is visible
    and can be refreshed by a re-claim, rather than fail either
    permissively-wrong or restrictively-wrong."""
    if not pid_alive(pid):
        return False
    if not expected_start_key:
        return True
    if not _is_recognized_start_key(expected_start_key):
        append_alarm(
            f"pid {pid}: recorded start_key is in an unrecognized or "
            f"legacy format (not {START_KEY_FMT!r}) — treating identity "
            f"as UNKNOWN and assuming the holder is still alive rather "
            f"than risking a false self-heal. Re-claim this lane once "
            f"it's safe to refresh the fingerprint to the current format."
        )
        return True
    current = proc_start_key(pid)
    if current is None or not _is_recognized_start_key(current):
        return True
    return current == expected_start_key


class CritSectionError(RuntimeError):
    """Raised by _CritSection when it cannot acquire the lock within its
    bounded wait, or when the lock path is not the directory this tool
    itself creates (finding #3, 2026-09-27 bug-gate). Caught by main()'s
    generic handler, printed clearly, exit 1 — never a silent hang."""


class _CritSection:
    """Short-lived mkdir-based lock around one lane's read-modify-write, so
    two concurrent `lane-lock claim`/`record-fail` calls on the same lane
    never interleave. NOT held for a build's duration — that is
    ws-build-slot's job. Same mkdir-atomicity idea as ws-build-slot, scaled
    down to a single critical section.

    Bounded wait, no exceptions: the old version, on a stuck lock, retried
    `rmdir()` forever with a bare `except OSError: pass`. If the lock path
    was a plain FILE (not the directory this tool creates — an external
    write, a backup restore, a tooling mistake), `rmdir()` always raises
    NotADirectoryError, which that bare except swallowed, so the loop spun
    forever with no error, no timeout, no visible symptom besides the
    caller hanging. This version (a) recognizes a wrong-type path
    immediately and fails loud instead of trying to fix it automatically,
    and (b) after exactly one steal attempt on a genuinely stale
    directory lock, raises instead of retrying without limit."""

    def __init__(self, lane_dir: Path):
        self.lockdir = lane_dir / ".critsec.lock"

    def __enter__(self):
        deadline = time.time() + LOCK_WAIT_S
        stole_once = False
        while True:
            try:
                self.lockdir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=False)
                return self
            except FileExistsError:
                if self.lockdir.exists() and not self.lockdir.is_dir():
                    # Wrong-type path: never auto-delete an unknown file at
                    # a lock location. rmdir() on this always raises
                    # NotADirectoryError, so looping here never converges —
                    # fail immediately and say exactly why.
                    raise CritSectionError(
                        f"lane-lock critsec path {self.lockdir} exists but "
                        f"is not a directory (wrong-type path — an "
                        f"external write or tooling mistake, not this "
                        f"tool). Refusing to touch it automatically; "
                        f"inspect and remove it by hand, then retry."
                    )
                if time.time() > deadline:
                    if stole_once:
                        # Already stole once this call and still can't get
                        # the lock — bounded wait means bounded, full
                        # stop, never a second unbounded retry loop.
                        raise CritSectionError(
                            f"lane-lock critsec lock {self.lockdir} did "
                            f"not clear after {LOCK_WAIT_S}s + one steal "
                            f"attempt — timing out instead of hanging. "
                            f"Retry, or inspect {self.lockdir} by hand."
                        )
                    try:
                        self.lockdir.rmdir()
                        stole_once = True
                    except OSError as e:
                        raise CritSectionError(
                            f"lane-lock critsec lock {self.lockdir} timed "
                            f"out after {LOCK_WAIT_S}s and could not be "
                            f"cleared ({type(e).__name__}: {e}) — exiting "
                            f"instead of hanging."
                        ) from e
                    continue
                time.sleep(0.05)

    def __exit__(self, *exc):
        try:
            self.lockdir.rmdir()
        except OSError:
            pass


def lane_dir(lane: str) -> Path:
    d = ROOT / lane
    d.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    return d


def read_json(path: Path, default: dict) -> dict:
    try:
        return json.loads(path.read_text())
    except (FileNotFoundError, json.JSONDecodeError):
        return dict(default)


def write_json_atomic(path: Path, data: dict) -> None:
    tmp = path.with_suffix(path.suffix + f".tmp{os.getpid()}")
    tmp.write_text(json.dumps(data, indent=2) + "\n")
    tmp.replace(path)


def append_alarm(line: str) -> None:
    try:
        ALARMS_PATH.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        with open(ALARMS_PATH, "a") as f:
            f.write(f"{now_iso()} [lane-lock] {line}\n")
    except OSError:
        pass  # alarm logging must never crash the caller


def owner_path(d: Path) -> Path:
    return d / "owner.json"


def breaker_path(d: Path) -> Path:
    return d / "breaker.json"


DEFAULT_BREAKER = {
    "fail_streak": 0,
    "tripped": False,
    "tripped_at": None,
    "history": [],  # last N {ts, ticket, gate, result, detail}
}


def read_breaker_failing_closed(d: Path) -> tuple[dict, bool]:
    """Read breaker.json for lane-dir `d`. Unlike read_json()'s generic
    "any parse failure returns the caller's default," a corrupt
    breaker.json must NEVER be indistinguishable from a healthy, untripped
    breaker (finding #2, 2026-09-27 bug-gate: that silent equivalence is
    exactly what let a corrupted file un-trip a breaker the hub had
    deliberately tripped). A missing file (never yet written) is still a
    normal, healthy default — only a file that EXISTS but fails to parse
    is treated as a safety event: fails CLOSED (as if already tripped),
    prints a loud warning, and appends one alarm line. Returns
    (breaker_dict, was_corrupt)."""
    path = breaker_path(d)
    try:
        text = path.read_text()
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return dict(DEFAULT_BREAKER), False
    try:
        return json.loads(text), False
    except json.JSONDecodeError as e:
        corrupt = {
            "fail_streak": DEFAULT_BREAKER["fail_streak"],
            "tripped": True,
            "tripped_at": now_iso(),
            "history": [],
        }
        msg = (
            f"lane={d.name} breaker.json is CORRUPT ({type(e).__name__}: "
            f"{e}) — failing CLOSED (treating the lane as TRIPPED) until "
            f"a human inspects and fixes or explicitly `reset`s it. This "
            f"is a safety fail-closed, never a self-heal."
        )
        print(f"WARNING: {msg}", file=sys.stderr)
        append_alarm(msg)
        return corrupt, True


def read_owner_failing_closed(d: Path) -> tuple[dict, bool]:
    """Read owner.json for lane-dir `d`. Mirrors read_breaker_failing_closed
    exactly, for the sibling file (2026-09-27 re-gate r2, blocker #2): a
    MISSING file (never claimed) is a normal, healthy 'unheld' state
    (returns ({}, False)). A file that EXISTS but fails to parse must
    NEVER be silently treated as 'unheld' — that is precisely how a
    corrupted ownership record would defeat this tool's entire purpose
    (mutual exclusion), the same defect class already fixed for
    breaker.json. Fails CLOSED instead: returns a sentinel
    {"_corrupt": True} and True, prints a loud warning, and appends one
    alarm line, every time it's detected. `force-release` is the only
    command that must NOT go through this — it's the designated recovery
    path and has to work even when the file can't be parsed at all."""
    path = owner_path(d)
    try:
        text = path.read_text()
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return {}, False
    try:
        return json.loads(text), False
    except json.JSONDecodeError as e:
        msg = (
            f"lane={d.name} owner.json is CORRUPT ({type(e).__name__}: "
            f"{e}) — failing CLOSED (treating the lane as held by an "
            f"UNKNOWN owner, refusing claims/release) until a human "
            f"inspects it or runs `lane-lock force-release {d.name} --by "
            f"<name> --reason <text>` to mechanically clear it. This is a "
            f"safety fail-closed, never a self-heal."
        )
        print(f"WARNING: {msg}", file=sys.stderr)
        append_alarm(msg)
        return {"_corrupt": True}, True


def cmd_claim(args) -> int:
    if args.pid is None:
        print(
            "lane-lock error: --pid is required for `claim`. Pass the pid "
            "of the actual long-lived caller (e.g. --pid $$ for the "
            "calling shell/session, or a build job's own pid) — never "
            "omit it. Omitting --pid used to silently record this "
            "short-lived CLI's OWN pid, which is already dead by the time "
            "the command returns, letting a second caller claim the same "
            "lane moments later with zero refusal (finding #1, 2026-09-27 "
            "bug-gate).",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return 1
    if args.pid <= 0:
        print(
            f"lane-lock error: --pid must be a positive integer (got "
            f"{args.pid}) — pass the real pid of the long-lived caller, "
            f"never a sentinel or placeholder value.",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return 1
    d = lane_dir(args.lane)
    with _CritSection(d):
        breaker, breaker_corrupt = read_breaker_failing_closed(d)
        if breaker.get("tripped"):
            corrupt_note = (
                " NOTE: breaker.json was found CORRUPT on this read and is "
                "being treated as tripped (fail-closed) — inspect the "
                "file, fix or accept the reset, and only then `reset` "
                "once real lane health is confirmed."
                if breaker_corrupt
                else ""
            )
            print(
                f"REFUSED lane={args.lane}: breaker TRIPPED at "
                f"{breaker.get('tripped_at')} (fail_streak reached "
                f"{breaker.get('fail_streak')}) — run `lane-lock reset "
                f"{args.lane} --by <name> --reason <text>` after the hub "
                f"has actually looked at the diff, not before.{corrupt_note}"
            )
            return 3

        op, owner_corrupt = read_owner_failing_closed(d)
        if owner_corrupt:
            print(
                f"REFUSED lane={args.lane}: owner.json is CORRUPT — held "
                f"by an UNKNOWN owner until a human resolves it. Run "
                f"`lane-lock force-release {args.lane} --by <name> "
                f"--reason <text>` to mechanically clear it, then claim "
                f"again."
            )
            return 2
        holder_is_live = bool(op) and pid_is_same_process(
            op.get("pid"), op.get("start_key")
        )
        held_by_other_live = (
            op and op.get("owner") != args.owner and holder_is_live
        )
        if held_by_other_live:
            print(
                f"REFUSED lane={args.lane}: held by '{op.get('owner')}' "
                f"(pid {op.get('pid')}, claimed {op.get('claimed_at')}, "
                f"reason: {op.get('reason', '')!r}) — still alive"
            )
            return 2

        # Either unheld, held by us already, or held by a dead/replaced
        # pid (self-heal).
        record = {
            "lane": args.lane,
            "owner": args.owner,
            "pid": args.pid,
            "start_key": proc_start_key(args.pid),
            "session": args.session or "",
            "claimed_at": now_iso(),
            "reason": args.reason or "",
        }
        write_json_atomic(owner_path(d), record)
        healed = (
            " (previous holder's pid was dead or reused by a different "
            "process — self-healed)"
            if op and not holder_is_live and op.get("owner") != args.owner
            else ""
        )
        print(f"CLAIMED lane={args.lane} owner={args.owner} pid={record['pid']}{healed}")
        return 0


def cmd_release(args) -> int:
    d = lane_dir(args.lane)
    with _CritSection(d):
        op, owner_corrupt = read_owner_failing_closed(d)
        if owner_corrupt:
            print(
                f"REFUSED release lane={args.lane}: owner.json is CORRUPT "
                f"— cannot verify you are the recorded owner. Run "
                f"`lane-lock force-release {args.lane} --by <name> "
                f"--reason <text>` instead."
            )
            return 2
        if not op:
            print(f"OK lane={args.lane}: already unheld")
            return 0
        if op.get("owner") != args.owner:
            print(
                f"REFUSED release lane={args.lane}: current owner is "
                f"'{op.get('owner')}', not '{args.owner}' — a peer can "
                f"never release another owner's claim, self-release only "
                f"(use `force-release` if the recorded owner can never "
                f"come back to release it itself)"
            )
            return 2
        owner_path(d).unlink(missing_ok=True)
        print(f"RELEASED lane={args.lane} by owner={args.owner}")
        return 0


def cmd_force_release(args) -> int:
    """Mechanical override for a lane stuck by pid reuse (finding #4,
    2026-09-27 bug-gate), a corrupt owner.json (blocker #2, 2026-09-27
    re-gate r2), or any other reason a normal `release` can't reach (the
    owner's process/session is gone and can never self-release, or the
    record can't even be parsed to find out who it was). Deliberately
    does NOT go through read_owner_failing_closed() — this command's
    entire job is to work even when the file is unreadable. Always logs
    who released it and why, the same way `reset` logs breaker
    overrides — never a silent unlock."""
    d = lane_dir(args.lane)
    with _CritSection(d):
        try:
            op = json.loads(owner_path(d).read_text())
            unreadable = False
        except FileNotFoundError:
            op = {}
            unreadable = False
        except json.JSONDecodeError:
            op = {}
            unreadable = True
        if not op and not unreadable:
            print(f"OK lane={args.lane}: already unheld (force-release was a no-op)")
            return 0
        prior_owner = op.get("owner", "(unknown)") if not unreadable else "(unknown — owner.json was corrupt)"
        prior_pid = op.get("pid", "(unknown)") if not unreadable else "(unknown)"
        owner_path(d).unlink(missing_ok=True)
    line = (
        f"lane={args.lane} FORCE-RELEASED by {args.by}: was held by "
        f"'{prior_owner}' (pid {prior_pid}) — reason: {args.reason!r}"
    )
    print(f"OK {line}")
    append_alarm(line)
    return 0


def cmd_status(args) -> int:
    d = lane_dir(args.lane)
    op, owner_corrupt = read_owner_failing_closed(d)
    breaker, breaker_corrupt = read_breaker_failing_closed(d)
    if owner_corrupt:
        print(
            f"lane={args.lane} owner=UNKNOWN (owner.json is CORRUPT — "
            f"failing closed, refusing claims/release; run `force-release "
            f"{args.lane} --by <name> --reason <text>` to clear it)"
        )
    elif op:
        same = pid_is_same_process(op.get("pid"), op.get("start_key"))
        if same:
            state = "alive"
        elif pid_alive(op.get("pid")):
            state = "DEAD (pid reused by a different process — will self-heal on next claim)"
        else:
            state = "DEAD (will self-heal on next claim)"
        print(
            f"lane={args.lane} owner={op.get('owner')} pid={op.get('pid')} "
            f"({state}) claimed_at={op.get('claimed_at')} reason={op.get('reason', '')!r}"
        )
    else:
        print(f"lane={args.lane} owner=(none, unheld)")
    if breaker.get("tripped"):
        corrupt_note = " (breaker.json was CORRUPT — failing closed)" if breaker_corrupt else ""
        print(
            f"lane={args.lane} breaker=TRIPPED at {breaker.get('tripped_at')} "
            f"fail_streak={breaker.get('fail_streak')}{corrupt_note}"
        )
    else:
        print(f"lane={args.lane} breaker=closed fail_streak={breaker.get('fail_streak', 0)}")
    return 0


def cmd_list(args) -> int:
    if not ROOT.exists():
        print("(no lanes yet)")
        return 0
    for d in sorted(ROOT.iterdir()):
        if not d.is_dir():
            continue
        args2 = argparse.Namespace(lane=d.name)
        cmd_status(args2)
    return 0


def _record_history(breaker: dict, ticket: str, gate: str, result: str, detail: str) -> None:
    breaker.setdefault("history", [])
    breaker["history"].append(
        {"ts": now_iso(), "ticket": ticket, "gate": gate, "result": result, "detail": detail}
    )
    breaker["history"] = breaker["history"][-20:]  # bounded, never grows unbounded


def cmd_record_fail(args) -> int:
    d = lane_dir(args.lane)
    with _CritSection(d):
        breaker, _corrupt = read_breaker_failing_closed(d)
        breaker["fail_streak"] = int(breaker.get("fail_streak", 0)) + 1
        _record_history(breaker, args.ticket, args.gate, "FAIL", args.detail or "")
        just_tripped = False
        if breaker["fail_streak"] >= THRESHOLD and not breaker.get("tripped"):
            breaker["tripped"] = True
            breaker["tripped_at"] = now_iso()
            just_tripped = True
        write_json_atomic(breaker_path(d), breaker)

    if just_tripped:
        msg = (
            f"lane={args.lane} BREAKER TRIPPED: {breaker['fail_streak']} "
            f"consecutive {args.gate} FAILs (ticket={args.ticket}, latest "
            f"detail={args.detail!r}) — further `lane-lock claim` on this "
            f"lane is refused until an explicit reset"
        )
        print(f"TRIPPED {msg}")
        append_alarm(msg)
        return 3
    print(
        f"OK lane={args.lane}: recorded FAIL #{breaker['fail_streak']} "
        f"(threshold {THRESHOLD}) for {args.gate} on ticket {args.ticket}"
    )
    return 0


def cmd_record_pass(args) -> int:
    d = lane_dir(args.lane)
    with _CritSection(d):
        breaker, _corrupt = read_breaker_failing_closed(d)
        breaker["fail_streak"] = 0
        _record_history(breaker, args.ticket, args.gate, "PASS", args.detail or "")
        write_json_atomic(breaker_path(d), breaker)
    still_tripped = breaker.get("tripped")
    print(
        f"OK lane={args.lane}: fail_streak reset to 0 for {args.gate} on "
        f"ticket {args.ticket}"
        + (" — breaker still TRIPPED, needs an explicit `reset`" if still_tripped else "")
    )
    return 0


def cmd_reset(args) -> int:
    d = lane_dir(args.lane)
    with _CritSection(d):
        breaker, _corrupt = read_breaker_failing_closed(d)
        was_tripped = breaker.get("tripped")
        breaker["tripped"] = False
        breaker["tripped_at"] = None
        breaker["fail_streak"] = 0
        breaker.setdefault("history", []).append(
            {"ts": now_iso(), "ticket": "", "gate": "reset", "result": f"RESET by {args.by}", "detail": args.reason or ""}
        )
        write_json_atomic(breaker_path(d), breaker)
    if was_tripped:
        line = f"lane={args.lane} breaker RESET by {args.by}: {args.reason!r}"
        print(f"OK {line}")
        append_alarm(line)
    else:
        print(f"OK lane={args.lane}: breaker was not tripped, fail_streak cleared anyway")
    return 0


def main() -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="lane-lock")
    sub = ap.add_subparsers(dest="cmd", required=True)

    p = sub.add_parser("claim")
    p.add_argument("lane")
    p.add_argument("--owner", required=True)
    # NOT argparse `required=True`: that would exit via SystemExit(2),
    # colliding with this tool's own exit-code-2 meaning ("held by another
    # live owner"). default=None + the explicit check at the top of
    # cmd_claim gives a clear message AND exit 1 ("usage/internal error"),
    # never silently defaulting to this CLI's own dying pid (finding #1).
    p.add_argument("--pid", type=int, default=None)
    p.add_argument("--session", default="")
    p.add_argument("--reason", default="")
    p.set_defaults(func=cmd_claim)

    p = sub.add_parser("release")
    p.add_argument("lane")
    p.add_argument("--owner", required=True)
    p.set_defaults(func=cmd_release)

    p = sub.add_parser("force-release")
    p.add_argument("lane")
    p.add_argument("--by", required=True)
    p.add_argument("--reason", required=True)
    p.set_defaults(func=cmd_force_release)

    p = sub.add_parser("status")
    p.add_argument("lane")
    p.set_defaults(func=cmd_status)

    p = sub.add_parser("list")
    p.set_defaults(func=cmd_list)

    p = sub.add_parser("record-fail")
    p.add_argument("lane")
    p.add_argument("--ticket", required=True)
    p.add_argument("--gate", required=True, choices=["bug-gate", "playtest", "other"])
    p.add_argument("--detail", default="")
    p.set_defaults(func=cmd_record_fail)

    p = sub.add_parser("record-pass")
    p.add_argument("lane")
    p.add_argument("--ticket", required=True)
    p.add_argument("--gate", required=True, choices=["bug-gate", "playtest", "other"])
    p.add_argument("--detail", default="")
    p.set_defaults(func=cmd_record_pass)

    p = sub.add_parser("reset")
    p.add_argument("lane")
    p.add_argument("--by", required=True)
    p.add_argument("--reason", required=True)
    p.set_defaults(func=cmd_reset)

    args = ap.parse_args()
    try:
        return args.func(args)
    except Exception as e:  # never crash unlabeled — always say what broke
        print(f"lane-lock error: {type(e).__name__}: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
